Z||000000||lennyb4||06-14-2000||01:14 PM||||I pay around 1,500 for full coverage per year for our fiero, MG Miget,and station wagon (the family grocery getter). Im just wondering what the average rate is that people pay in different area's.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by lennyb4 (edited 06-14-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||06-14-2000||01:33 PM||||I pay $130 per year for insurance and yes it is full coverage on the Fiero. It's listed as a pleasure car with less than 1000 miles per year. Of course during the winter it is stored in my garage. (It's $120 every 6 months while I drive it and $9.00 every 6 months while it is stored) If I were to drive it year round it would be $240 per year.<BR>Total for all 3 vehicles is $1180 per year.<BR>(86 Fiero, 94 Saturn, and 98 Durango)<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||06-14-2000||01:34 PM||||Full coverage for my 1992 Mustang GT Convert, 1985 Fiero 2M4 & 2000 Explorer XLT (all at State Farm) is $973.00 a year....In Illinois  ||||reg||14
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||06-14-2000||06:00 PM||||Well, the older I get the lower the insurance quote is.. when I got the $3,000 a year quote I was 19, but I just turned 20 today.. so now it should be a bit less than that. if I can find a cheap 4cyl coupe to play with, I'll get another quote on that.<P>I have no tickets, and yet my insurance is almost as high as a friend who has many tickets... doesn't seem fair.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||06-14-2000||08:20 PM||||Steve--The ages where the rates drop (at least here in the States) are:  21, 25, 28 (no idea why), 35, and 42.  Also, they get cheaper if you're married.  Even cheaper yet if you own your own home.  The insurance probably won't be affected too much by the model of Fiero.  I had full coverage on the Monkeymobile ('86 2M4) and currently have the same on the Grape Ape ('85 GT).  My rate only went up by $3US/month.  Not that you're planning on driving a truck, but I get a 10% discount because I have a CDL.  That's the only reason I kept it.  You might check into that.  If you have access to someone with a truck who could teach you to drive and you could read up enough to pass the written tests, you could save $300CDN/year.  Might not seem like much right now, but once you have a CDL, it's fairly easy to keep it and it would save you tons in the long run.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-14-2000||08:21 PM||||BTW--Happy Birthday!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||06-14-2000||09:06 PM||||I pay about 40 a month for my 86 GT, and 60 a month for my 97 T-bird (my 94 Super Coupe was about the same).  I still keep full coverage on both, with $250 deductible, and well above the legal minimums.  I'm 32, my wife is 31, no accidents in 6 years, no tickets in 8 years, and our life and homeowners policies are with the same agent.  State Farm, since 1988.  I'm in semi-rural SE Georgia, but we're considered a suburb of Jacksonville, FL.<BR>When I bought my Fiero in 1988, I was 20 and single, in the Navy in Norfolk, VA.  It cost around 150 a month then, but when I turned 21 two months later, it dropped to 100.  It dropped almost in half when I got married a year later.  <P>Sometimes I guess it's good to get older, but my rates really haven't changed overall since 1989.  The younger guys seem to just pay more.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||06-14-2000||09:58 PM||||With a $1000 deductible on each of 3 cars (2 pleasure only Fieros) and a 99 Buick GS it costs this 50+ person $130+ /month.||||reg||1
